WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:07.000
A novel virus causes panic, stinging insects&nbsp;
swarm America, and riots in the streets pit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.000
the people vs. police — it happened in 2020,&nbsp;
and "The Simpsons" knew about it all in 1993.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.400
"Yoo-hoo!"

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.120
Cartoon Network's "Johnny Bravo" followed the&nbsp;
buff blond womanizer through various exploits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.360
adventures and mishaps in one of&nbsp;
the best kids' shows of the 1990s.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.080
"If Santa doesn't get these letters by tonight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.880
I might not get all those&nbsp;
free presents I asked for!"

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.040
In the episode "Chain Gang Johnny,"&nbsp;
which aired on April 27, 2001,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.600
Johnny goes to the movies with his acquaintance&nbsp;
Carl. The two get into an argument over the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.520
and if you look to Carl's side, you'll&nbsp;
notice an image of a building on fire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.320
As the scene pans over, we see the words&nbsp;
"Coming Soon" plastered on the poster.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.320
"Oh, you will pay for this."

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.520
Seeing how the episode aired less than five&nbsp;
months before September 11, 2001, some have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.560
come to see the cartoon as an eerie vision of&nbsp;
the future. The actual answer is likely a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.360
anticlimactic. Buildings on fire are nothing new&nbsp;
to the world of action movies, and it's likely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.840
image merely functioned as an homage to something&nbsp;
like "The Towering Inferno." It's also worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.160
pointing out there's only one building depicted&nbsp;
in the advertisement instead of the two buildings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.720
that would make for the Twin Towers. So if you&nbsp;
were thinking this was a real prediction, well…

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.280
"Close." "Nice try, though."

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.880
In addition to "Johnny Bravo," "The&nbsp;
Simpsons" has also found itself at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.360
the center of "9/11 prediction" theories&nbsp;
thanks to one scene from a classic episode.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.840
"Stupid city. What the hell is this thing?"

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.720
In Season 9's "The City of New York vs. Homer&nbsp;
Simpson," the family decides to travel to the Big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.520
Apple to retrieve Homer's car. When discussing&nbsp;
how they could possibly afford such a trip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.080
Lisa holds up a magazine promotion, saying&nbsp;
how there are rides to New York for just $9.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.160
Right next to the price is the&nbsp;
silhouette of the Twin Towers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.840
It almost looks as though the cover&nbsp;
reads "9 11." It's enough to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.120
you want to get a hold of the Simpsons&nbsp;
writers, and ask them how they knew.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.360
"Here's a better idea: You give me your address,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.120
and I'll write to you." "Okay, just&nbsp;
send it to Jesus, care of the Pentagon."

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.080
It seems like all it takes to make a "9/11&nbsp;
prediction" is two towers that look similar and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.040
an airplane. That's precisely what you have with&nbsp;
the classic Bugs Bunny cartoon, "Falling Hare."

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.680
In it, Bugs goes toe-to-toe with a Gremlin on the&nbsp;
side of his plane. It's up to Bugs to navigate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.760
vessel to a safe landing, which becomes all&nbsp;
the more difficult when he finds himself in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.040
the midst of a massive city. At one point, the&nbsp;
rascally rabbit approaches two tall buildings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.840
extremely close to one another. At the&nbsp;
last second, before Bugs would make impact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.000
he turns the plane on its side so that he&nbsp;
safely flies between the two structures.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.080
Considering this cartoon came out decades&nbsp;
before the World Trade Center was built,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.400
we're thinking this is less of a "prediction,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.000
and more of a weird thing to notice&nbsp;
while you're watching old cartoons.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.520
"The Simpsons" has seemingly predicted everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.280
from Donald Trump's presidency to&nbsp;
Richard Branson going to space.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.400
But one of the show's most spot-on predictions&nbsp;
came in the form of the Season 5 episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.720
"$pringfield (Or, How I Learned to Stop&nbsp;
Worrying and Love Legalized Gambling)."

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.520
"Legalized gambling is a bad idea. You&nbsp;
can build a casino over my dead body."

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.960
In the episode, Springfield&nbsp;
becomes its very own Atlantic City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:21.280
complete with a towering casino with live&nbsp;
entertainment. One of these shows is "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.560
Flamboyant Magic of Gunter and Ernst," in what is&nbsp;
a clear parody of performers Siegfried and Roy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:30.960
who had a long-running show in Las Vegas for&nbsp;
many years with their signature white tigers.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.440
Gunter and Ernst also have a tiger by the&nbsp;
name of Anastasia, and after a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.040
when the tiger remembers what it was like&nbsp;
to live in the tranquility of the jungle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.760
it attacks the two performers. The&nbsp;
scene took on new life when in 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.160
Roy suffered severe injuries from a&nbsp;
tiger attack in real life. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.400
he managed to pull through from the incident,&nbsp;
and the duo would go on to perform again.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.301
"You know, we are having a party tonight."

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.600
Some cartoon predictions aren't super on the nose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.280
but they can still have a chilling&nbsp;
detail that makes you stop and say...

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.720
"Oh… my… god."

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:16.400
That's the case with a Season 8&nbsp;
episode of "American Dad!" where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:19.840
Steve and his friends stumble upon a&nbsp;
downed plane. Once venturing inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.880
they find a corpse clutching a script, which&nbsp;
just so happens to be for "Fast &amp; Furious 7."

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.320
"Look, it's signed by Randy Scott Knobson, the guy&nbsp;
who wrote all the 'Fast and the Furious' movies!"

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.800
The guys are stoked to get a glimpse into&nbsp;
the future of the high-octane franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.520
but when viewing the episode today, it's hard&nbsp;
not to be reminded of Paul Walker's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.280
whose final, posthumous film&nbsp;
role was in "Furious 7."

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.840
Walker died tragically in a car crash, eerily&nbsp;
reminiscent of the plane crash depicted in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.800
episode. As it just so happens, the scene the&nbsp;
friends read from the script heavily involves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.560
Walker's character Brian O'Conner to make&nbsp;
the connection all the more heartbreaking.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.400
It's pretty impressive when a cartoon&nbsp;
predicts a single aspect of the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:07.280
It becomes downright bizarre when a single episode&nbsp;
contains multiple references to an entire year.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.920
Season 4's "Marge in Chains" starts off&nbsp;
with virtually everyone in Springfield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:16.480
coming down with a case of a mysterious&nbsp;
new disease known as the Osaka Flu.

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:16.640
"The dreaded Osaka Flu has hit Springfield&nbsp;
with over 300 cases now reported."

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.000
The novel disease spread from Japan&nbsp;
after some factory workers coughed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.000
into a box on its way to Springfield.&nbsp;
Despite the fact this virus came from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.400
Japan and coronavirus initially hailed&nbsp;
from China, many couldn't help but see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.960
some parallels -- especially considering&nbsp;
how the rest of the episode plays out.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.440
Later in the episode, the citizens of the small&nbsp;
town become panicked and turn toward Dr. Hibbert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.440
for guidance. He tells them that he doesn't&nbsp;
have anything that can cure the outbreak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:47.520
and in an ensuing clash, people knock over&nbsp;
a box of killer bees. Bees aren't hornets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.480
but it still reminded people of the murder&nbsp;
hornets that briefly overtook headlines in 2020.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:58.960
Eventually, the episode culminates in mass social&nbsp;
unrest, with protesters knocking down statues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:04.080
and facing off with police in the&nbsp;
streets. Gee, that feels familiar.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.160
"This never would've happened&nbsp;
if Marge Simpson was here."

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.800
Kevin Spacey's fall from grace may have been a&nbsp;
little more predictable to people in Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.360
than it was to the rest of the world. After&nbsp;
decades of critically-acclaimed performances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.600
the actor was accused by several men&nbsp;
of sexual harassment and assault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.480
sending his career into a nosedive. But&nbsp;
the news may not have been too surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.680
for anyone who enjoyed watching&nbsp;
early episodes of "Family Guy."

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.400
In the season four episode "Don't Make Me&nbsp;
Over," Brian and Stewie make a wager, ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:39.680
resulting in Stewie running naked through&nbsp;
a mall shouting something weirdly specific.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:44.080
"Help! I've escaped from Kevin&nbsp;
Spacey's basement. Help me!"

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:48.320
Apparently, "Family Guy" creator Seth&nbsp;
Macfarlane has a knack for identifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:53.120
sexual predators. Before Harvey Weinstein&nbsp;
was outed as a criminal sexual predator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.200
Macfarlane made a joke at the producer's&nbsp;
expense during an Oscar-related telecast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.160
After announcing the nominees for&nbsp;
Best Supporting Actress, he joked:

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.720
"Congratulations, you five ladies no longer have&nbsp;
to pretend to be attracted to Harvey Weinstein."

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:08.880
With that kind of batting average,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.440
maybe Macfarlane should work less with&nbsp;
cartoons and lean into detective work.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.880
"What have we got today, boys?" "Well, it&nbsp;
seems we've got a bit of a crime spree."

